<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Komenda Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1700">1700</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komenda">Komenda</a> Wars were a series of conflicts from 1694 to 1700 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> kingdom of Eguafo and its port of Komenda. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> and the English Royal African Company backed rival local rulers and allies, including the merchant John Cabess, in a struggle for control of the trade in gold and slaves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/komenda-wars.jpg" alt="Komenda Wars" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G. Child · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forts_at_Komenda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and the English had factories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komenda">Komenda</a>. In 1687 the French negotiated with the king of Eguafo to open one too, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> used force to make the king expel them. In the fighting of 1688 the Twifo joined the Dutch and won trade concessions, the king of Eguafo was killed, and a prince allied to the Dutch, Takyi, took the throne. Komenda fell largely under the Dutch and the Twifo, and Takyi, growing uneasy with both, tried to balance them with English interests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>There were four wars. The merchant John Cabess, a loyal ally of the Royal African Company whose goods the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> had seized in 1684, invited the English back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komenda">Komenda</a> in November 1694 and attacked Dutch miners outside the town. In February 1695 his forces attacked the Dutch Fort Vredenburgh, and on 28 April the Twifo were defeated. On 26 June, during negotiations, the Dutch chief Willem Bosman tried to shoot Cabess; seizures of people, called panyarring, went on until a short peace late in 1695.</p><p>On 21 January 1696 a young prince, Takyi Kuma, began a civil war for the throne with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> support, but he was beaten on 20 March, and the new Dutch governor Jan van Sevenhuysen made peace with Eguafo. In 1697 the English paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> as much as the Dutch had offered them, keeping them neutral. By November 1698 the English had turned against Takyi, killed him in an attack and backed Takyi Kuma with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from Asebu, Cabess Terra and Akani, who were routed. A fourth war from November 1699 ended on 9 May 1700, when Takyi Kuma was named king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Little territory changed hands, but the English came out with the main advantage in trade on the coast. They soon alienated Takyi Kuma by demanding repayment of debts, and his death in 1704 brought another civil war. The wars and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> outbreak in the early 1700s depopulated the coast, and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and panyarring became common practice later in the century. The disorder helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> expand and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a>. Willem Bosman's journals, published in 1703, are the main account.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Komenda Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 17th century the Dutch, English and others competed for its favour, and the <b>Komenda Wars</b> from 1694 to 1700 drew Eguafo into conflict between the Dutch and English companies.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-eguafo">Kingdom of Eguafo</a></li></ul></section>
